# Default: normalize every text file to LF in the repository AND in the working
# tree, on every platform. Without this, a Windows checkout inherits Git for
# Windows' core.autocrlf=true and silently gets CRLF working copies of files
# nothing pins below (*.tcl, *.md, *.theme, *.test). That matters here because rio
# PRESERVES a file's line endings on save (AGENTS.md D22) — so a source file that
# flips to CRLF once stays CRLF, and extension payloads and test fixtures would
# carry endings the Linux hosts never see. Pinning it makes the checkout identical
# everywhere regardless of the contributor's autocrlf setting.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts must check out with CRLF line endings: Windows PowerShell 5.1
# mis-parses some constructs (notably here-strings) in LF-only files. Force native
# Windows endings so a Windows checkout always gets a script PowerShell can parse.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The POSIX shell scripts stay LF everywhere.
*.sh  text eol=lf

# What a RELEASE tarball leaves out. `export-ignore` is honoured by `git archive`,
# which is what GitHub runs to build the "Source code (zip/tar.gz)" assets on a
# Release page — so these are absent from the download while staying in the
# repository, where they belong. It changes the artifact, NOT the project: a clone
# still gets every one of them, and so does the Code tab. Anything that truly
# should not be public has to be `git rm`'d instead, and even then history keeps it.
#
# The line is: a tarball is for someone RUNNING rio, a clone is for someone
# WORKING on it. So the development record goes — the ADRs (AGENTS.md remains, it
# is the log those records formalise), the O1 Ck probes, the agent definitions —
# and so do the test suites, which are the evidence a contributor needs and dead
# weight to everyone else.
#
# Checked before adding these: nothing under docs/ links into any of them, so the
# manual's cross-references (D100 — they reach README, INSTALL, WINDOWS, CAVEATS,
# LICENSE, CONTRIBUTING and AGENTS, all of which stay) still resolve in a tarball.
# Note that `rio-gui/tests/docs.tcl` cannot catch a regression here: it runs in a
# repository, where every target exists either way.
#
# CONTRIBUTING.md is the one document left pointing at what is gone — it tells you
# to run `rio-core/tests/all.tcl`. That is accepted rather than fixed: CONTRIBUTING
# addresses people who clone, and export-ignoring it too would break the manual's
# own links to it, which is the worse trade.
adr/           export-ignore
spike/         export-ignore
tests/         export-ignore
.claude/       export-ignore
.gitattributes export-ignore
